window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"20-28","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"20-28-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-01/","title_he":"א – שלא ללכת בחוקות הגויים","title_en":"Not Following the Customs of the Gentiles","body_en":"<p>In many verses the Torah warned Israel not to follow the customs (ḥukot) of the gentiles, as it is stated (Vayikra 18:3): \"You shall not copy the practices of the land of Egypt where you dwelt, nor shall you copy the practices of the land of Canaan to which I am bringing you, nor shall you follow their customs.\" It is likewise stated after the section on the forbidden sexual relations (Vayikra 20:22-24): \"You shall keep all My statutes and all My laws and do them, so that the land to which I am bringing you to dwell in will not spew you out. You shall not follow the customs of the nation that I am driving out before you, for they did all these things and I loathed them. And I said to you: You shall possess their land, and I will give it to you to possess, a land flowing with milk and honey; I am the Lord your God, who has separated you from the peoples.\" It is likewise stated (Shemot 23:24): \"You shall not bow down to their gods nor serve them, nor shall you act according to their practices…\". And it is likewise stated (Devarim 12:30): \"Beware lest you be ensnared to follow them after they have been destroyed before you, and lest you inquire about their gods, saying: How did these nations serve their gods? I too will do the same.\"</p>\n<p>The meaning of the prohibition is that one should not try to imitate the gentiles in their customs in order to be like them, for example by dressing or cutting one's hair according to the garments and hairstyles that are distinctive to them, so that Israel will not be drawn after the culture and faith of the gentiles and abandon the mitzvot of the Torah. A 'ḥok' is a custom that has become so fixed that it has turned into a binding practice. In other words, beyond the prohibition against worshipping idols or honouring them, there is a prohibition against following the customs of the gentiles, that is, against resembling the gentiles by adopting customs that have become fixed in their culture.</p>\n<p>Included in the prohibition is not to resemble the gentiles by performing acts based on superstitions. An example is cited in the Talmud (Shabbat 67b): there were gentiles who used to say to a raven when it called, \"Scream!\", and to a female raven, \"Whistle, and turn your tail back to me for good.\" If they heard a rooster that called with the voice of a raven, or a hen that called with the voice of a rooster, they saw this as a bad sign and ordered that it be slaughtered. Likewise, one who drank a beverage would say, \"I will drink and leave over, I will drink and leave over,\" as a charm so that drinking a little would benefit him like much. Similarly, people who raised chicks used to hurl eggs against a wall as a charm for the chicks' survival, and there were women who for this purpose danced before the chicks and counted seventy-one chicks. There were also customs of women connected to cooking food: when they prepared a dish of kutaḥ (a dip made with whey) they danced before the dish so that it would succeed; when they cooked lentils they silenced the people so that they would not spoil the cooking; when they cooked groats they shrieked so that they would cook well; and there were some who used to urinate before the dish so that it would succeed.</p>\n<p>In the language of the Sages this prohibition is called 'the ways of the Emori' (Shabbat 67a-b), after the peoples of Canaan, who were also called by the general name 'Emorites'. Similarly, anyone who practises a gentile custom that has no logical explanation or proven benefit, so that it is reasonable to assume that it is based on idolatrous customs or on a superstitious belief that this custom will bring luck, transgresses the prohibition of \"nor shall you follow their customs\" and is liable to lashes.<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The prohibition is called 'the ways of the Emori', and it is derived from the verses cited above (in Avoda Zara 11a and Ḥullin 77a, Rashi cited as the source the verse from Shemot 23:24, and in Shabbat 67a he cited Vayikra 18:3; the prohibition of gentile dress was derived by the Sifrei from Devarim 12:30). According to the Yere'im 313, the prohibition of 'their customs' applies only to the customs mentioned by the Sages in tractate Shabbat 67b and in Tosefta Shabbat chapter 7, \"and one should not add to them.\" It has been explained that the Yere'im follows his own view that the prohibition is to imitate the seven peoples of Canaan and Egypt, and since they have ceased to exist, there are no additional forbidden customs. Some say that the prohibition is to imitate idol worshippers (Yad Peshuta on Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:1; see Da Ma She-tashiv 1:8). However, according to most poskim, the prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles' includes the customs of all gentiles in all generations, and the words of the Sages are merely examples of forbidden customs (this is the basic assumption of all the poskim who discussed in their responsa all gentiles. So wrote Kli Yakar, Vayikra 18:3, and Mayim Ḥayim by Rav Ḥayim David Halevi 3:227. This also emerges from the many poskim who wrote that the prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles' applies to Muslims even though they do not worship idols, among them: Rivash 158; Tashbetz 3:133; Pri To'ar 4:11)."}]},{"slug":"20-28-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-02/","title_he":"ב – גדרי האיסור","title_en":"The Parameters of the Prohibition","body_en":"<p>The prohibition is to learn from the gentiles customs that have no benefit, and whose entire reason for existing is that they are customs that became a statute of a foreign religion, or are done for the sake of licentiousness and arrogance (Maharik 88; BY and Rema YD 178:1).</p>\n<p>Even with a gentile custom that is not connected to a foreign religion or to licentiousness, anyone who imitates it because he wishes to resemble the gentiles transgresses the prohibition (see the note).<sup>2</sup></p>\n<p>But if the gentiles' custom had a reason, it is permitted to do it, since one does not do it because it is a gentile custom but because of the logical benefit that may come from it. Therefore it was permitted to load stones onto trees that were shedding their fruit, because people held that by doing so the tree's strength would be weakened and it would have no surplus strength with which to shed its fruit. Likewise it is permitted to mark such trees with red paint, so that passers-by will see that they are sick and pray for them. Likewise it is permitted to do anything that people who practised medicine were accustomed to do for the sake of healing, even though these appear to be charm-like customs without reason, since in practice they are effective in the opinion of the healers (Shabbat 67a; below 29:1).</p>\n<p>Similarly, the Sages permitted burning the personal articles of a king who died, even though the gentiles had this custom, because this custom has a reason – to express honour for the king who died, in that there is no other person worthy of using his articles (Avoda Zara 11a).</p>\n<p>Similarly, the Rivash (158) permitted mourners to leave their homes and go to the cemetery every day, as was the custom of the Muslims, since this custom has a reason: it is done in honour of the deceased.</p>\n<p>Likewise, the Maharik (88) permitted physicians to wear a special garment that the gentile physicians were particular to wear, so that patients would know that they were physicians and come to be treated by them for payment. Even though this was a gentile custom, and Jewish physicians in the past did not follow it, since the Jewish physicians wished to adopt it for the sake of their livelihood, there is no prohibition in this.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"In Responsa Maharik 88 he wrote that the prohibition of 'their customs' applies only when the custom is one of a foreign religion or of licentiousness and 'haughtiness and pride', and many poskim cited him as halakha. Seemingly, according to this there is no prohibition to imitate gentiles in order to resemble them with a custom that is not connected to a foreign religion or to licentiousness and arrogance. But this is difficult, for the poskim imply that the very desire to imitate gentiles is also forbidden, as emerges, for example, from the discussion of the hairstyles of secular gentiles (below, section 3 and note 5, in the words of Leḥem Yehuda 2:95). It seems that the Maharik too would agree with this, for he wrote that a custom of 'haughtiness and pride' is forbidden by Torah law; consequently, when a Jew seeks to look like a gentile in a conspicuous and distinctly gentile matter, he is trying to act arrogantly and to say that he is not like the 'old-fashioned' or 'lowly' or 'religiously zealous' Jews, but rather prides himself on looking like a gentile, and this is forbidden by Torah law even when there is no licentiousness in it. However, the dispute arose when the Jews claimed that they did not wish to imitate the gentiles but rather that this was attractive in their eyes. Those who were stringent argued that since they had learned it from the gentiles, they really wished to imitate them, whereas the lenient accepted their claim, and although they did not consider it proper for them to be influenced by the gentiles, since they did not say that they wished to resemble them, they held that there was no prohibition in it.<br>Another possibility is that imitating the gentiles in something that is not a custom of a foreign religion is forbidden rabbinically, as the Meiri wrote (Sanhedrin 52b): by Torah law it is forbidden to follow customs connected to a foreign religion, and following other customs is forbidden by the words of the scribes, \"so that they will keep their distance from them and from their idols and not be drawn after them from one thing to another.\" So wrote Rabbi Avraham son of the Rambam (Ha-maspik Le-ovdei Hashem, part 2, 25), and so emerges from the Responsa of the Rambam (244). So wrote Shiyarei Knesset Ha-gedola (178, Hagahot Beit Yosef 5). See Keren Le-David 22 and Da Gezeirat Oraita p. 19."},{"n":3,"en":"In Avoda Zara 11a we learned that it is permitted to burn the clothes and personal articles of a king who died, and there is no prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles' in this, \"rather it is a mark of importance.\" The Ran explained (there 2b in the pages of the Rif): \"The Torah forbade only the customs of idolatry – these are things of vanity and emptiness, and all of them involve a need of idolatry; but things that have a reason are permitted. And in burning for kings there is a reason: to burn their personal articles in their honour, to say that no other person is fit to use what he used.\" This is also the view of the Maharik (shoresh 88), who explained at length that the prohibition applies to things that show that the Jew intends by his actions to resemble the gentiles who do these acts – such as things that have no reason, which he would not do were it not that he wished to imitate the gentiles; or things that involve licentiousness or arrogance, whose performance expresses adoption of their culture. In Sanhedrin 52b the Sages added that burning a king's personal articles also has a source in Scripture, as it is stated (Yirmiyahu 34:5): \"You shall die in peace, and as there were burnings for your fathers, the former kings who were before you, so shall they burn for you.\" That is, the verse teaches that there is a reason and a benefit to the practice (Melamed Le-ho'il OḤ 16; Seridei Esh 2:39).<br>However, the Gra (YD 178:7) disagreed with the Ran and the Maharik, and in his opinion even something that has a reason may not be learned from the gentiles unless it has a source in the Torah, for so emerges from Sanhedrin 52b, where execution by the sword required a source from the Torah even though it is logical. Some Aḥaronim derived this view from Tosafot (Sanhedrin 52b, s.v. \"ela\"), who inferred from the Gemara there that the permission to burn the king's articles is because it has a source in a verse, implying that without a source in a verse, even a logical custom may not be learned from the gentiles (Maharam Schick, Sanhedrin 52b). Some hold that this is also the view of the Ran (Mishpetei Uziel 7:60; Yayin Ha-tov part 1, YD 12). However, Seridei Esh (2:39) explained, based on Tosafot in Avoda Zara (11a, s.v. \"ve-i\"), that the Ba'alei Ha-tosafot too agree with the Ran and the Maharik, and there is no need to say that the passages of Gemara are in dispute.<br>In practice, the overwhelming majority of poskim wrote that there is no prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles' when the practice has a reason and a benefit. So wrote the Rivash (158), the Baḥ (YD 178:7) and the Maharikash (Erekh Leḥem 178:1). BY 178:1 and the Rema there also ruled like the Ran and the Maharik. So wrote Levush 178:1; Imrei Esh YD 55; Beit Shlomo YD 2:227; Ktav Sofer YD 175; Yad Shaul; Yad Ha-ketana; Pri Ha-sadeh 1:48; Beit Hillel; and many others. Even according to the Gra, when the custom has a tangible and clear reason and benefit, there is no prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles' in it, for he wrote: \"And not necessarily because it is written in the Torah, but anything that we would do even without them – is permitted\" (Seridei Esh 2:39). For example, it is obvious that even according to the Gra it is permitted to eat with cutlery even though this is a custom that originated among the gentiles, since its benefit is very clear."}]},{"slug":"20-28-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-03/","title_he":"ג – חוקות הגויים בתספורת","title_en":"The Customs of the Gentiles in Hairstyles","body_en":"<p>It is forbidden to cut one's hair in a 'komi' style as idol worshippers used to do, who would shave the hair at the front of the head and grow it long at the back. Likewise it is forbidden to shave the hair around the head and leave the hair in the middle of the head. And anyone who cuts his hair in a manner distinctive to the gentiles transgresses the prohibition of \"nor shall you follow their customs\" and is liable to lashes (Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:1; SA YD 178:1). However, the Sages permitted a Jew who needed to be close to the government to have a 'komi' haircut and to dress like them, so that he could persuade them not to issue decrees against the Jews subject to them, for if he did not look like them they would despise him, and he would not succeed in acting on behalf of Israel (Bava Kamma 83a; SA YD 178:2).<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>Some say that it is forbidden for men to grow long hair for the sake of adornment and pride, because this is an imitation of a gentile custom (Baḥ), for there were warriors in various countries who used to grow their hair long (Taz). And there were rabbis who warned young men against imitating other hairstyles that were widespread in the gentile society around them. On the other hand, there were those who defended those young men, holding that since they did not intend to imitate the gentiles but rather found it attractive, even though they learned it from the gentiles, there is no prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles' in it. In addition, it is always a matter of a hairstyle that is not prevalent among all the gentiles in that place, and on the other hand there are also Jews who customarily wear it.</p>\n<p>Several generations ago, many students of the Lithuanian yeshivot, and especially of the Slabodka school, were particular about their dignified appearance, so that it would look attractive even in the eyes of those who had left the Torah. To this end they wore a short modern suit in place of the long coat that had been customary until then, and they shaved their beards and grew a forelock above their foreheads and combed it according to the accepted fashion. When the Slabodka yeshiva moved to the Land of Israel (5684, 1924), there were members of the Old Yishuv who came out forcefully against them, claiming that they were influenced by modernity and were transgressing the Torah prohibition of following 'the customs of the gentiles'. But many held that there is no prohibition in this, because it is not a distinctly gentile custom that expresses religious or national affiliation, but rather this was how respectable young men conducted themselves at that time. Moreover, the heads of the yeshivot of the Slabodka school held that this dress had an advantage, for in a generation in which many young people were abandoning the way of the Torah and despising yeshiva students, it was fitting that yeshiva students should appear attractive and respectable in the eyes of all people.</p>\n<p>In practice, any hairstyle that is also accepted among Jews and is not considered a distinctly gentile hairstyle involves no prohibition. When there is a distinctly gentile hairstyle, Jews who imitate it in order to resemble the gentiles transgress the prohibition of \"nor shall you follow their customs\". But if many Jews transgressed and adopted this hairstyle, since it has become prevalent among Jews too, it is no longer defined as a distinctly gentile custom, and the prohibition no longer applies to it.</p>\n<p>As a rule, even when there is no outright prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', it is proper for a person not to imitate people who do not observe Torah and mitzvot, because the dress and hairstyle characteristic of those who observe Torah and mitzvot help him to walk in his path and to avoid foreign influences and passing fashions. In addition, there is Kiddush Hashem in a Jew declaring by his appearance that he is loyal to the Jewish people and its heritage.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Sifra (Aḥarei Mot, parasha 8, 13:9): \"And why does the Torah say: 'nor shall you follow their customs'? … Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira says: That you shall not naḥor [?], nor grow a forelock, nor cut your hair komi at the edge.\" According to Rashi (Sota 49b, s.v. \"mesaper\"), 'blorit' (forelock) and 'komi' are names for the same hairstyle, in which idol worshippers would shave the hair at the front and grow a kind of braid at the nape of the neck for the sake of idolatry. Many Rishonim explained likewise. According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:1), blorit and komi are two different hairstyles, as explained above (BY).<br>The Gemara explains (Bava Kamma 83a) that they permitted Avtolmos bar Reuven to cut his hair komi because he was close to the government, and so it was ruled as halakha (Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:3; SA YD 178:2). BY 178:2 asked: how could they permit a Torah prohibition? He answered: (a) for the sake of saving Israel the Sages have the power to permit; (b) the Torah did not define the prohibition but left this to the Sages, and they determined that there is no prohibition for those close to the government. The Baḥ 178:7 explained that the Torah prohibition applies when one wishes to resemble the gentiles, but when he does not wish to resemble them but needs to cut his hair like them because of the need to save Israel from their hands, there is no prohibition. Tosafot (Bava Kamma 83a, s.v. \"hitiru\") wrote that \"from the outset they did not decree upon those close to the government.\" It seems that their view is like the second answer in BY or like the Baḥ (see Seridei Esh 2:39)."},{"n":5,"en":"Many explained that the Rambam's intention (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:1) is to forbid any kind of hair growing in the manner of the gentiles (Baḥ 178:4; Taz 1; Prisha 3; Shakh 1). The Taz explained: \"For the rule is that Israel must be separate from them; what difference does it make whether this way or that way.\" Similarly, the Tashbetz wrote (3:93) that one should be careful not to shave the pe'ot (sidelocks) in the manner of the gentiles, even when this involves no prohibition of using a razor or of 'you shall not round off'. The Beit Yosef (178:1) also agreed that one should not imitate the hairstyle of the gentiles. Leḥem Yehuda 2:95 added that when the intention is for adornment and not to resemble the gentiles there is no prohibition, but if their intention is that they should not be distinguishable among the gentiles so that they can socialise [lit. \"play\"] with them, this is an outright prohibition (cited in Ikarei Ha-dat YD 19:4 and in Zivḥei Tzedek 178:3). In Ruaḥ Ḥayim (by Rabbi Ḥayim Palagi, 182:4) he wrote that although it is impossible to forbid by strict law ornamental hairstyles like those of the gentiles, it is proper to make a fence against this, because these hairstyles lead to sin. Yeḥaveh Da'at (2:2) and Aseh Lekha Rav (7:81) wrote similarly. However, in the book Da Gezeirat Oraita, published in Jerusalem in 5691 (1931), the opinions of rabbis were collected who forbade by Torah law the custom of dress and shaving of the students of the Slabodka and Ḥevron yeshivot, because the desire to beautify oneself like the gentiles is forbidden as 'the customs of the gentiles', and also because the hair constitutes an interposition (ḥatzitza) for tefillin, and growing it also involves the prohibition of 'he shall not wear' [a woman's garment]. So wrote Minḥat Yitzḥak 8:4. On the other hand, many wrote that there is no Torah prohibition in this, because growing hair for adornment is not considered a gentile custom. It seems that even most of the rabbis who opposed their custom from an educational standpoint held that the custom involved no outright prohibition. Moreover, after many Jews conducted themselves this way, there is no longer a prohibition of the customs of the gentiles in it (see Beit She'arim YD 236; Yeḥaveh Da'at 2:2; Bnei Banim part 1, 6-7).<br>Some are stringent and hold that even one who shaves his beard in a way that does not transgress the prohibition of the razor transgresses 'their customs', since he is imitating the gentiles (see Hadrat Panim Zakan part 2, chapter 4, pp. 438-441). According to the Ḥatam Sofer (OḤ 159), after some Jews began to shave with permission because of a decree of the gentiles, there is no longer any prohibition in the matter for any Jew. In practice, for two reasons there is no prohibition: (a) whenever the aim is beauty and not imitation, according to Leḥem Yehuda and Ruaḥ Ḥayim there is no prohibition; (b) even when the first ones intended to imitate and transgressed the prohibition, once the custom spread and became common, the prohibition no longer applies to it (Beit Shlomo YD 197). I wrote likewise above, and so emerges from the custom of the Slabodka students."}]},{"slug":"20-28-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-04/","title_he":"ד – חוקות הגויים במלבושים","title_en":"The Customs of the Gentiles in Clothing","body_en":"<p>Included in the prohibition of \"nor shall you follow their customs\" is not to dress according to the custom distinctive to gentiles, and the prophet warned (Tzefanya 1:8) that those who do so will be punished, as it is stated: \"And I will punish the officials and the king's sons, and all who wear foreign garments.\" The Sages said that Israel was redeemed from Egypt in the merit of not changing their dress (Pesikta Zutarta, Ki Tavo 46a). The Sages likewise expounded (in the Sifrei) on the verse (Devarim 12:30): \"Beware lest you be ensnared to follow them after they have been destroyed before you, and lest you inquire about their gods…\": \"That you should not say: Since they go out in avtiga, I too will go out in avtiga; since they go out in purple, I too will go out in purple; since they go out in tulsin, I too will go out in tulsin\" (Rambam, Sefer Ha-mitzvot, negative commandment 30).</p>\n<p>We do not know what the garments mentioned by the Sages looked like, but it is agreed in halakha that it is forbidden to wear two kinds of gentile garments: the first, garments that gentiles customarily wear as part of their religious traditions, such as garments distinctive to the clergy of the various religions, as well as garments that members of religions customarily wear on their festivals, such as garments in special colours at Christmas or green garments on St Patrick's Day. The second kind is garments that gentiles customarily wear for the sake of licentiousness and arrogance, such as red purple garments in days gone by (Sifrei Devei Rav; Maharik; Rema YD 178:1).</p>\n<p>But there is no prohibition for Jews to wear garments that the gentiles wore first, when the aim is not imitation but comfort or beauty. Therefore it is permitted for Jews to wear fashionable clothing, since they do so for the sake of beauty. However, in places where the Jews set themselves apart from the dress of the gentiles, one who changes the dress of the Jews in order to resemble the gentiles transgresses a Torah prohibition (Ḥokhmat Adam 89:1). But if they did not decide to set themselves apart from a particular garment, it involves no prohibition. For example, it is permitted for university students to wear a special gown at a graduation ceremony.</p>\n<p>Even when Jews initially decided to set themselves apart from the gentiles regarding a certain garment, if Jews began to wear it, although the first ones transgressed the prohibition, once this garment has become prevalent among Jews there is no longer a prohibition in it (Beit Shlomo YD 197).</p>\n<p>Inspired by the prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', many rabbis and educators warn their students not to imitate various gentile customs even though they do not fall within the bounds of the Torah prohibition, such as excessive devotion to sports teams and wearing their items of clothing, each community according to its circumstances.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"Some say that even by wearing a single gentile item one transgresses the prohibition (Divrei Ḥayim YD 1:30; Erekh Shai YD 178:1). And some say that the prohibition applies only when one's overall appearance is an imitation of the gentiles (Beit Shlomo YD 197; Mishneh Halakhot 4:115:2, who wrote that this also emerges from the view of the Maharik).<br>Some say that even when Jews are forced to wear gentile garments, it is forbidden to wear them because of \"nor shall you follow their customs\" (Imrei Esh YD 55). And some say that since they wear them against their will, there is no prohibition (Beit Shlomo YD 197).<br>It seems that wearing the clothing of sports teams or gentile singers is not included in the prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', since there are also Jews who wear these garments (based on Beit Shlomo YD 197). However, since in practice this involves identification with gentiles and their values, it poses an educational problem whose consequences could be grave, akin to the consequences that the prohibition of 'their customs' addresses.<br><b>When the custom has already taken root in Israel:</b> Beit Shlomo YD 197 wrote that although the first ones who began dressing like gentiles transgressed the prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', once the dress has become prevalent among Jews there is no longer a prohibition in it, since it is no longer distinctive to gentiles. However, some hold that if they began with a prohibition, because they wished to resemble the gentiles, then even later, when the custom spread in Israel, the prohibition continues as at the beginning (Meshiv Ba-halakha 46, branch 4; Kav Ha-ḥayim 1, s.v. \"akh\", regarding the bima of the synagogue. Several books also sought to derive this from Responsa Ḥatam Sofer 1:159. According to Divrei Ḥayim YD 1:30, where the custom has already spread it is permitted, but in other places it is forbidden). But in practice it is accepted to rule like Beit Shlomo. Likewise we find in many responsa on various matters of 'the customs of the gentiles' that one of the important reasons for permitting is that the custom has already spread in Israel (Yayin Ha-tov by Rav Yitzḥak Nissim part 1, YD 12; Igrot Moshe OḤ 4:66; Yabi'a Omer part 3, YD 25; and more). And although they did not speak explicitly about a situation that began with a prohibition, since at first this custom had not yet spread in Israel, it is clear that the first ones transgressed the prohibition, and only afterwards did they permit it because it had spread in Israel. Thus, in practice, any custom practised by many Jews cannot be considered forbidden because of 'the customs of the gentiles'."}]},{"slug":"20-28-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-05/","title_he":"ה – כיפה","title_en":"Kippa","body_en":"<p>Some say that where Jews live among gentiles, a man who goes without a kippa transgresses the prohibition of \"nor shall you follow their customs\" (Mahari Bruna 34). On the other hand, according to many, those who go without a kippa do not transgress the prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', because the gentiles go without a head covering for reasons of comfort alone. Therefore going without a kippa is not a 'ḥok' of theirs.</p>\n<p>However, all agree that it is right to be very particular about wearing a kippa, for wearing it strengthens religious identification and arouses the fear of Heaven, and there is also Kiddush Hashem in this, for one who wears a kippa thereby declares that it is an honour for him to be regarded as a faithful Jew.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"In Responsa Mahari Bruna 34 he wrote that although in the days of the Talmud wearing a kippa was a measure of piety, nowadays, among Jews who live abroad among the gentiles, the kippa is the identifying mark that they are Jews, and therefore those who go without a kippa are considered to be transgressing the prohibition of \"nor shall you follow their customs\". So wrote the Taz OḤ 8:3; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav OḤ, Mahadura Tinyana 2:6. The Taz added that going bareheaded also has a religious marker, for Christians customarily remove their hats on entering church. On the other hand, according to Mateh Efrayim (Hilkhot Berakhot 7), since gentiles customarily remove their hats also for reasons of comfort, a Jew who does so is not imitating gentiles, and therefore does not transgress the prohibition of 'their customs'. So wrote the author of Ḥavot Ya'ir in the book Mekor Ḥayim (2:10); Bekhor Shor (Shabbat 118b); Neta Sorek (OḤ 2); Yabi'a Omer (9, OḤ 1:5).<br>In gentile schools it was strictly customary to sit bareheaded without hats. According to Naḥalat Binyamin 30, it is forbidden to obey this instruction because of the prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles'. Whereas according to Melamed Le-ho'il 2:56, since in their view removing the hat is for the sake of respect and courtesy, there is no prohibition in this. He wrote that Rabbi Samson Raphael Hirsch instituted this in the school in Frankfurt: during Torah studies they wore a head covering, and during secular studies they removed it, so as not to offend the non-Jewish teachers. And in Igrot Moshe (YD 4:11:3) he wrote that nowadays even the Taz would agree that going bareheaded does not involve 'the customs of the gentiles', since today it is clear that gentiles go without a kippa for reasons of comfort and not as a religious statute, for even the secular among them go that way; therefore he permitted going without a kippa for the sake of livelihood. However, regarding praying or entering a synagogue bareheaded, he wrote (OḤ 4:40:14) that it is a custom of the gentiles, since this is how Christians conduct themselves when they pray."}]},{"slug":"20-28-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-06/","title_he":"ו – שמות נוכרים","title_en":"Gentile Names","body_en":"<p>Some say that it is forbidden by Torah law to call children by gentile names that are not found among Jews, and likewise one whose parents gave him two names, one gentile and one Jewish, is forbidden to use the gentile name. For the Torah forbade resembling gentiles even in their dress and customs, and all the more so one should not resemble them in their names. We likewise learned from the words of the Sages (Bamidbar Rabba 13:20) that Israel was redeemed from Egypt in the merit of the fact \"that they did not change their names, and did not change their language, and that they fenced themselves off from sexual immorality.\" But according to most poskim, although it is not proper to call a Jew by a distinctly gentile name, this does not involve an outright prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', since it is not done for the sake of a foreign religion or for the sake of licentiousness and arrogance (above, section 2). Therefore there is also no obligation for one who has two names, Jewish and gentile, to make sure that he is called specifically by the Jewish name.</p>\n<p>All this is when the name is a distinctly gentile one that is not found among Jews, but if the gentile name has already taken root among Jews, according to all opinions there is no objection to using it. Indeed, we find that in all the exiles Jews were called by gentile names, among them rabbis and rabbis' wives, such as Rabbi Alexandri (Berakhot 17a), Rabbi Maimon the father of the Rambam, and Rabbi Vidal the author of the Maggid Mishneh. Likewise in Yiddish: Fishel, Ber, Gitel, Golda. And likewise in Sefarad: Fortuna. And although ideally it would have been proper for Jews to be called by names rooted in the Hebrew language, and in the opinion of many, the first ones who began calling their children by the gentile name did not act properly, once the name has taken root in Israel there is no objection to calling children by this name.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"The Sages said (Bamidbar Rabba 13:20) that Israel was redeemed from Egypt in the merit of the fact \"that they did not change their names, and did not change their language, and that they fenced themselves off from sexual immorality.\" Following on from this, the Maharam Schick (YD 169) was stringent and wrote that one who calls someone by a gentile name transgresses the prohibition of \"nor shall you follow their customs\", for just as it is forbidden to resemble the gentiles in their dress, so it is forbidden to resemble them in their names. His student, the Maharshag (2:194), explained that the prohibition applies only when one intends to resemble them.<br>However, in the Talmud and in the words of the Rishonim no prohibition against using gentile names is mentioned, and in the baraita in Gittin 11b it is even stated that \"most Jews outside the Land – their names are like the names of idolaters.\" So wrote the Maharashdam YD 199, regarding the Portuguese anusim (forced converts) who had received gentile names, and when they returned to Judaism asked whether they could use the gentile name for business purposes and the like; he answered that although it is a measure of piety to avoid being called by a gentile name, there is no prohibition in it. So is implied by the Maharit Tzahalon (Ha-ḥadashot 158). And so wrote many, that it is not good to call Jews by gentile names, but they did not write that there is a prohibition in it, among them: Ḥatam Sofer 4, EH 2:38; Yeshu'ot Malko OḤ 12; and others. And when the foreign name is a translation of the Jewish name, such as Tzvi Hirsch, Aryeh Leib or Dov Ber, they saw no problem in it.<br>When it is a name that has already taken root in Israel, according to all opinions there is no prohibition to call a Jew by this name. As we find that Tanna'im, Amora'im, Rishonim and Aḥaronim were called by gentile names, such as Antigonos of Sokho, Symmachus, Rabbi Alexandri, and others, as explained above. And although when people began using these names, it is possible that there were attempts to prevent it and protests against it, from the time the names took root in Israel there is no objection to using them (Igrot Moshe OḤ 4:66; 5:10; EH 3:35)."}]},{"slug":"20-28-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-07/","title_he":"ז – עוגב ושינוי מנהגים בבית הכנסת","title_en":"The Organ and Changing Customs in the Synagogue","body_en":"<p>On Sukkot of the year 5579 (1818), a 'Temple' was established in Hamburg, Germany, which later proved to have been the first Reform synagogue. At that time many German Jews, especially the young, were no longer careful about observing Shabbat and kashrut. The founders of the 'Temple' decided to adapt the prayers to the spirit of the young people of the modern generation, who had integrated into German society and did not understand Hebrew. To this end they shortened the prayers, removed passages relating to the sacrificial service, translated many passages into German, shortened the reading of the weekly Torah portion to a third, with the intention of completing the Torah in three years, and abolished the reading of the haftara. They also decided to accompany the prayers with organ playing by a gentile on Shabbat. They claimed that all the changes of custom they made were within the framework of halakha. But the dayanim of the city, and with them great rabbis from all over Central Europe, such as Rabbi Akiva Eiger and Rabbi Moshe Sofer, came out sharply against them, both because they were transgressing halakha and because of the grave concern that these changes would be the beginning of a disastrous process. About twenty-five years later, in 5604 (1844), the Reform movement took shape, which no longer purported to act within the framework of halakha but claimed that the mitzvot of the Torah that did not suit the spirit of enlightened German culture should be abolished. In those years in Germany, at least nine out of ten young people did not continue in the way of Yisrael Saba (the ancestral tradition of Israel), and most German Jews identified with the Reform movement (Masoret Ha-giyur p. 336).</p>\n<p>As stated, in the first stage the dispute was whether the changes that the innovators introduced in the 'Hamburg Temple' were within the framework of halakha. In the following generations the dispute gradually spread to synagogues in other countries whose worshippers had been influenced by the Reformers but still wished to remain within the framework of halakha. The two stormy disputes were over bringing an organ into the synagogue and moving the bima to the front of the synagogue. Since one of the central arguments against these changes was that they involve the prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', we will mention them here briefly.</p>\n<p>The organ is a musical instrument with a rich and moving sound, which Christians play in churches, and the Jewish innovators decided to accompany the prayers with it. In support of their initiative they cited the custom of the worshippers of a certain synagogue in Prague, who even before the dispute broke out used to accompany Kabbalat Shabbat with music. In addition, there was testimony about other synagogues in Italy and Frankfurt that did the same. They argued that although this custom was learned from the Christians, there is no prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles' in it, since it is done in order to enrich the prayers and not in order to resemble them. In addition, the organ is not specific to Christian prayer and is used for other things as well. And since the aim is to enrich the prayers and to draw the young generation closer to prayer in the synagogue, there is a mitzva in it, and consequently it is permitted to ask a gentile to play on Shabbat, since this is a prohibition of 'shevut de-shevut' (a rabbinic prohibition upon a rabbinic prohibition), which is permitted for the sake of a mitzva.</p>\n<p>But the vast majority of rabbis opposed very forcefully bringing an organ into the synagogue. Some held that it is forbidden by Torah law because of 'the customs of the gentiles', and some did not claim this, but held that from an educational standpoint it is forbidden to be drawn after the customs of the gentiles. In addition, since there is no mitzva in it, it is forbidden to ask a gentile to play on Shabbat. They further added that since the day the Temple was destroyed, where the Levites played music, it is not proper to create a substitute for their music in synagogues.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, a question arose for rabbis whose communities demanded that they bring an organ into the synagogue, and if they did not comply, Reform rabbis would be appointed in their place. In practice, in places where there was concern about the influence of the Reformers, most rabbis instructed the rabbis of those communities to resign and not to agree to bringing in an organ (Rav Hildesheimer; Melamed Le-ho'il 1:16). However, Rav Mordechai Horowitz wrote that since if he resigned a rabbi would come who would permit far worse things, \"I cannot take upon myself and upon my neck the responsibility for all the mishaps that will result from this, Heaven forbid\" (Mateh Levi OḤ 6). In Algeria, Rav David Ashkenazi did not abolish the custom, practised even before his time, of playing the organ in the great synagogue of Oran, and he wrote that it is better to fight over more important matters such as sheḥita and gittin (bills of divorce).<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"In Responsa Ḥatam Sofer part 6, Likkutim 84, 86, he explained that playing the organ is forbidden because of 'the customs of the gentiles', as well as because of the prohibition of 'shevut', and more. So wrote very many rabbis, among them: Mishpetei Uziel part 8, OḤ 18; Seridei Esh 1:16; Yaskil Avdi part 8, OḤ 29. In Responsa Rabbi Azriel Hildesheimer OḤ 187 he wrote that one should not even pray in a place where the organ is played, because of 'the customs of the gentiles'. However, a question arose whether a rabbi who is pressed to do so by the congregation, and who, if he does not comply, will be dismissed and replaced by a rabbi who leans towards the Reformers, must resign. Rav David Tzvi Hoffmann in Responsa Melamed Le-ho'il 1:16 discussed this at length and wrote that in order not to lend a hand to a breach that will widen, he must resign, and he noted that four rabbis with whom he shared the question agreed with him. However, Rav Mordechai Horowitz in Responsa Mateh Levi OḤ 6 did not decide that he should resign. In 5695 (1935) Maran Ha-rav Kook was asked from Oran in Algeria whether the prohibition against bringing in an organ, instituted by the Ashkenazi rabbis, also applies to Sephardi communities, and he answered that the prohibition is the same for all, because of 'the customs of the gentiles' or because of imitating the innovators, and on Shabbat also because of the prohibition of 'shevut' (Oraḥ Mishpat OḤ 36). In response, the rabbi of the city, Rav David Ashkenazi (later the chief rabbi of Algeria, and the father of Rav Yehuda Léon Ashkenazi, 'Manitou'), was indignant at the intervention of Rav Kook, who did not know the place; from his words it emerges that this was the practice even before him, and he chose not to quarrel over it so as to rectify more important things, and this is a matter left to the decision of the local rabbi. In my humble opinion, Maran Ha-rav Kook too would have agreed with this, had the questioner given him the full information. Even before the question arose in Hamburg, under the influence of modernity there were those who used to play the organ in various places in Italy and in Prague, and there were rabbis who agreed to this, among them: Rav Avraham Yosef Shlomo Graziani (of Italy, born 5380 [1620]), in his glosses to SA OḤ 560:3; Rav Ya'akov Ḥai Recanati (Italy, born 5523 [1763]). And Rav David Pardo (5478-5550 [1718-1790]), in Responsa Mikhtam Le-David OḤ 19, wrote that there is no prohibition to play music on Yom Tov in honour of the Torah."}]},{"slug":"20-28-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-08/","title_he":"ח – שינוי מקום הבימה בבית הכנסת","title_en":"Changing the Location of the Bima in the Synagogue","body_en":"<p>The custom of Israel is to place the bima on which the Torah is read in the centre of the synagogue, so that everyone can hear the ḥazan well (Rambam, Hilkhot Tefila 11:2-4). However, Rabbi Yosef Karo wrote (Kesef Mishneh there) that this is not obligatory, and when there is a need, it may be placed at the side, and \"everything depends on the place and the time.\"</p>\n<p>The Reformers used to place the bima at the front of the synagogue, similar to churches, in which all the ceremonies take place at the front of the church. Many rabbis came out against changing the location of the bima, and wrote that even Rabbi Yosef Karo permitted changing the location of the bima only in a new synagogue, but where the bima had already been fixed in the past in the centre of the synagogue, the bima has acquired its place and it is forbidden to move it (Ḥatam Sofer OḤ 28; Imrei Esh OḤ 7). Later, people became more careful to keep away from the customs of the Reformers and feared that any change might undermine the foundations of religion, and even in a new synagogue, and even when there was a constraint, they did not agree to fixing the bima at the front of the synagogue (Meḥolat Ha-maḥanayim OḤ 5; Meshiv Davar OḤ 15). And there is one who explained that whenever there is space between the bima and the Holy Ark, such that one can circle the bima with the four species, there is no prohibition (Igrot Moshe OḤ 2:41).</p>\n<p>Some ruled that it is preferable to pray alone than in a synagogue where the bima was moved forward (Maharam Schick OḤ 71; Yaskil Avdi part 7, OḤ 9). But according to many, one should not forbid praying in the synagogue because of this (Seridei Esh part 1, OḤ 15; Igrot Moshe OḤ 2:42). Moreover, in Muslim lands, where the Reformers had no influence, people were not particular about this matter, and when there was a need they placed the bima at the front of the synagogue (Yabi'a Omer part 8, OḤ 17). In practice, nowadays, when all the communities of Israel are intermingled, in case of need, such as in small synagogues, one may place the bima at the front of the synagogue, because by doing so one does not appear to be imitating the gentiles.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-28-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-09/","title_he":"ט – שינויים במנהגי החופה","title_en":"Changes in Wedding Customs","body_en":"<p>Another change that the Reformers introduced was holding wedding ceremonies (ḥuppot) in the synagogue, like the Christians who held weddings in churches. Indeed, in the days of the Rishonim there were also Jews who used to hold ḥuppot in the synagogue (see Rema YD 391:3), but since over the years this custom ceased, and it appeared that the Reformers had introduced it in imitation of the Christian custom, the rabbis of Hungary rose up against it and forbade holding ḥuppot in the synagogue because of the prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles' (Ktav Sofer EH 47; Maharam Schick EH 87; Meḥolat Ha-maḥanayim 4). And some forbade it because of the affront to the honour of the synagogue, which is designated for prayer and Torah alone (Imrei Esh OḤ 9). In consequence, other rabbis outside Hungary tended to forbid holding ḥuppot in the synagogue. However, in many communities in Muslim lands it was customary to hold ḥuppot in the synagogue.</p>\n<p>In practice, when there is no intention to imitate the gentiles, it is permitted to hold a ḥuppa in the synagogue, on condition that the honour of the synagogue and the customs of modesty are preserved (Ta'alumot Lev part 3, 57; Igrot Moshe EH 1:93; Yabi'a Omer part 3, EH 10).</p>\n<p>Later, following the rise of the feminist movement, the Reformers began to institute that at the ḥuppa the bride too would put a ring on the groom. But Rav Feinstein wrote that this involves a prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', for this is the Christian custom. In addition, people may mistakenly think that the kiddushin (betrothal) is effected by this (Igrot Moshe EH 3:18). On the other hand, some hold that this does not involve a prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', since it is not done in order to imitate the gentiles but to express the bride's partnership in the wedding. And although there is no prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles' in it, it is right not to change the custom, and if the bride wishes to give the groom a ring, she should give it to him after the ḥuppa (the Rebbe Rayatz, Igrot Kodesh 8, 2284; Dibrot Eliyahu 12:158). It is worth noting that many God-fearing men customarily wear a wedding ring from the time of their marriage, in order to express that they are married and thereby to preserve their modesty.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-28-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-28-10/","title_he":"י – שינויים במנהגי הלוויה","title_en":"Changes in Funeral Customs","body_en":"<p>Another dispute arose regarding the funeral procession. The accepted custom was to place the deceased on a bier (stretcher) and to carry the bier by hand to the cemetery, and there were those who changed this and placed the bier on a black carriage, as was the custom of the gentiles. Many wrote that since the cities had grown and consequently the way to the cemetery had become longer, there is a reason for changing the custom, and therefore it involves no prohibition (Ktav Sofer YD 175). But there was one who forbade changing the custom and conveying the deceased in a carriage because of 'the customs of the gentiles' (Tuv Ta'am Va-da'at, Telita'ah 2:235). And there was one who forbade it when the carriage is black, like that of the gentiles (Beit She'arim YD 427).</p>\n<p>Another dispute arose over planting trees and flowers around the grave. Some rabbis ruled that it is forbidden because of 'the customs of the gentiles' (Rav Hildesheimer; Har Tzvi YD 279). And some rabbis forbade it because it changes the custom (Melamed Le-ho'il 2:109; Beit She'arim YD 428).</p>\n<p>Likewise, some forbade placing wreaths of flowers on the bier of the deceased or on the grave (Yaskil Avdi 4, YD 25). And some hold that there is no prohibition in this, since it is done in honour of the deceased and not in order to imitate the gentiles (Aseh Lekha Rav 1:44, and Responsa Mayim Ḥayim 3:27). And some agreed that there is no prohibition in it, but wrote that it is preferable not to change the custom and imitate the gentiles (Yabi'a Omer part 3, YD 24). Rav Goren did not protest against the military practice of laying wreaths, since this is done in all the armies of the world as a mark of honour and not as religious imitation; however, outside the framework of the army he wrote that it is not proper to lay wreaths (Terumat Ha-goren 84).<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"In recent generations, as in our own, many disputes have arisen around new customs that those inclined towards modernity adopted while changing and abolishing the previous custom. The disputes took place between the religious and the Reformers, and between conservative religious Jews and modern religious Jews. The changes of custom greatly pained many of those faithful to tradition, and when the changes were learned from the gentiles, they argued that they were forbidden because of 'the customs of the gentiles'. But viewed comprehensively, it seems that the innovators did not wish to imitate the gentiles but rather wished to adopt customs that they saw among the gentiles and that were attractive in their eyes. On the contrary, they often held that by adopting them they would strengthen Judaism and prevent many from turning after the gentiles. Therefore, one who holds that the innovators' desire to change stems from the influence of gentile culture should rightly base his opposition on the principle that one should not be influenced by gentile culture, and explain the conceptual difference between the cultures and the customs, but he should not accuse those who make the changes of a Torah prohibition of 'the customs of the gentiles', since their aim is to honour the Jewish religion. Likewise, one who holds that the benefit in the matter does not justify changing the custom should rightly oppose the change on the grounds that a custom should not be changed without great need. In this way the dispute will be precise and will not fan the flames of controversy.<br>I will mention further disputes around this subject. <b>Celebrating a birthday:</b> Some rabbis saw this celebration as an imitation of a gentile custom, and although the desire to hold the party is not in order to imitate the gentiles, since this custom has no root in Israel they held that it should not be held (Arugat Ha-bosem OḤ 2:216). But in practice one may hold a birthday party, and if it leads to soul-searching and good deeds, it even has merit (Ben Ish Ḥai, Year 2, Re'eh 17).<br><b>Bat mitzva celebration:</b> Some forbade holding it since the Reformers learned it from the Protestant Christians, who used to hold a ceremony of acceptance of the religion in adolescence. See Tzitz Eliezer 18:33. But in practice there is no prohibition, since today it is clear that the intention is not to imitate the gentiles but to add joy. And when one intends to hold the celebration in order to inspire the girl to rejoice in accepting the mitzvot, it is a mitzva, as many rabbis wrote, and as explained in Peninei Halakha, Likkutim 1, 2:11.<br><b>Standing for the siren:</b> Opposition also arose to standing for the siren on Memorial Day for the Fallen of Israel's Wars and on Holocaust Remembrance Day, because of 'the customs of the gentiles'. But in practice, since standing for the siren is meant to honour the holy ones of the Holocaust and the fallen of Israel's wars, not only is there no prohibition in it, but it even has merit, as explained in Peninei Halakha, Zemanim 4:13.<br>See further in Peninei Halakha, Mo'adim 13:13, on the custom of decorating the synagogue with branches on Shavu'ot, which the Gra forbade because of 'the customs of the gentiles', but according to halakha it is permitted since it has a reason."}]}]});
